<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Crusade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1147">1147</a>–<a href="/years/1149">1149</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> was launched after the Zengid capture of Edessa in 1144, ending the County of Edessa's capital. Preached by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a>, it was led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-iii-of-germany">Conrad III of Germany</a>, both of whose armies suffered heavy losses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Their joint siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1148 failed after four days.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-crusade.jpg" alt="Second Crusade" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Colombe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BattleOfInab.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> stood in the east, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and the County of Edessa, with the County of Tripoli added in 1109. Edessa was the most northerly, weakest and least populated, and was exposed to attack from Artuqid, Danishmendid and Seljuk neighbours. Zengi, atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1128. News of Edessa's fall reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1145, and Pope Eugene III issued the bull Quantum praedecessores on 1 December. It met a poor response and was reissued on 1 March 1146. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a> preached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> at Vézelay on 31 March 1146, where Louis VII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a> took the cross, and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where Conrad III received it at Speyer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The German army of about 20,000 men left in May 1147 and the French in June, both going overland through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and Byzantine territory. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a> feared an attack and posted troops, and the Germans, after a clash at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> quickly. Later sources, notably Odo of Deuil, accuse Manuel of having the Turks attack the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>, but this is likely an invention of Odo. Both armies were separately defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. Louis reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> on 19 March 1148 but refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-of-poitiers">Raymond of Poitiers</a>'s request to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, preferring to finish his pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><p>On 24 June 1148 the High Court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> met at Palmarea near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> and chose to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, a former ally of Jerusalem. Historians have long called this inexplicable folly, though David Nicolle defends it, arguing that Damascus was the most powerful Muslim state in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. The Crusaders arrived at Darayya on 23 July 1148 and were pushed back from the orchards by the defenders and Nur ad-Din. They moved to a less defended eastern plain with little food and water. The local lords refused to continue, and the army retreated on 28 July, harassed by Turkish archers all the way.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In the east each side felt betrayed by the other, and the distrust lingered for a generation, to the ruin of the Christian kingdoms. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> was successful: crusaders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> in June 1147 and helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso I of Portugal</a> take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> after a siege from 1 July to 25 October, and they also helped conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sintra">Sintra</a>, Almada, Palmela and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setubal">Setúbal</a>. Louis left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1149, and he and Eleanor, barely speaking, boarded separate ships in April 1149. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a>, humiliated, wrote an apology to the pope attributing the failure to the sins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Crusade” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Second Crusade</b> the Knights Templar and the Knights Hospitaller gained property in Croatia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-croatia-1102-1526">Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its foundation inspired repeated expeditions from Europe, including the <b>Second Crusade</b>, the Third Crusade led by Richard I and Philip II of France, and the crusades of Frederick II and Louis IX of France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The new kingdom expanded south at the expense of al-Andalus: with the help of English, Flemish and German crusaders bound for the <b>Second Crusade</b>, Afonso took Lisbon after a siege in 1147, and Santarém fell the same year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The sultanate repeatedly defeated passing crusaders: Kilij Arslan I destroyed the expeditions of 1101, and Mesud I’s forces routed the German army of Conrad III near Dorylaeum during the <b>Second Crusade</b> in 1147.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its fall prompted the <b>Second Crusade</b>.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Second Crusade</b>, the armies of Louis VII of France and Conrad III of Germany, with that of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, laid siege to Damascus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-24">24 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
